(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["phy-11-03"] = {"phy-11-03-m01":{"idea":"On a position-time graph, the steepness of the line tells how fast the position changes.","steps":["Velocity = change in position / change in time = Δx/Δt.","On an x–t graph, this ratio is the slope of the line.","For a curve, the slope of the tangent gives the velocity at that instant."],"others":"Acceleration is the slope of the velocity-time graph, not the position-time graph. Distance covered is a reading of the graph, not its slope. Average speed needs total distance over total time, not a slope at a point.","trap":"Slope of x–t is velocity. Slope of v–t is acceleration.","answer":"velocity at that instant"},"phy-11-03-m02":{"idea":"The area under a v–t graph is velocity multiplied by time, which is displacement.","steps":["A thin strip of the graph has the area v × dt.","v × dt is the small displacement in that time.","Adding all the strips gives the total displacement."],"others":"Average speed is a ratio, not an area. Momentum needs the mass and is not shown by this graph. Acceleration is the slope of the graph, not the area under it.","trap":"Area below the time axis counts as negative displacement.","answer":"displacement in that time interval"},"phy-11-03-m03":{"idea":"Average velocity is the total displacement divided by the total time.","steps":["The body goes 60 km east, then 60 km west. It is back at the start.","Net displacement = 0.","Average velocity = 0 / 2 h = 0."],"others":"The first leg alone gives 60 km/h east, but the whole trip must be used. 120 km/h and 60 km/h use the distance, which gives the average speed, not the average velocity.","trap":"Distance gives speed. Displacement gives velocity.","answer":"zero, as the displacement is zero"},"phy-11-03-m04":{"idea":"Speed uses distance and velocity uses displacement. They are equal if the path is straight with no turn.","steps":["Average speed = total distance / time.","Magnitude of average velocity = size of displacement / time.","Distance equals the size of displacement only when the body does not change direction on a straight line."],"others":"On a plane with constant acceleration, the path can curve, so distance exceeds displacement. A full circle gives zero displacement but a large distance. Turning back once makes the path longer than the displacement.","trap":"Any turn makes distance larger than the size of the displacement.","answer":"moving along a straight line without turning back"},"phy-11-03-m05":{"idea":"The equations of motion are derived with the acceleration a taken as a constant.","steps":["v = u + at comes from a = (v − u)/t, with a fixed.","s = ut + ½at² comes from the average velocity (u + v)/2, which is true only for steady a.","If a changes, these formulas no longer hold."],"others":"They hold in any direction, not just vertical. They also work if the body does not start from rest, with u not zero. They do not need a constant velocity, since the velocity changes when a is not zero.","trap":"Check first: is a constant? If not, integrate instead.","answer":"the acceleration is constant"},"phy-11-03-m06":{"idea":"Use v² = u² + 2as for a stopping distance, with a negative for retardation.","steps":["Final speed v = 0, u = 20 m/s, a = −5 m/s².","0 = 20² + 2(−5)s, so 10 s = 400.","s = 40 m."],"others":"80 m comes from a wrong factor of 2. 20 m is the speed, not the distance. 100 m comes from using u²/a² or a similar slip.","trap":"Check: average speed is 10 m/s and the time is 4 s, so the distance is 40 m.","answer":"40 m"},"phy-11-03-m07":{"idea":"Stopping distance goes with the square of the speed, since s = u²/2a.","steps":["With v = 0: s = u²/(2a).","For the same a, s is proportional to u².","Speed 2v gives (2)² = 4 times the distance: 4s."],"others":"2s would be true if s were proportional to the speed, but it goes with the square. s/2 goes the wrong way, since a faster car needs more distance. 8s would need the cube of the speed.","trap":"Double the speed means four times the stopping distance. This is why speed limits matter.","answer":"4s"},"phy-11-03-m08":{"idea":"A body falling from rest has u = 0, so the distance depends on t².","steps":["s = ut + ½gt².","With u = 0, s = ½gt².","So s is proportional to t²."],"others":"t³ is not found in any equation of constant acceleration. √t would mean that the fall slows down. A linear t would mean a constant speed, but the speed grows steadily.","trap":"In 2 s a body falls 4 times as far as in 1 s.","answer":"t²"},"phy-11-03-m09":{"idea":"At the top, the ball is changing direction. The velocity is zero but gravity still acts.","steps":["Going up, the velocity falls to zero.","At the top, v = 0 for an instant.","Gravity acts all the time, so the acceleration is g, downwards."],"others":"The velocity is zero at the top, not g upwards. The acceleration does not become zero, because gravity does not switch off. g acts downwards, not upwards.","trap":"Zero velocity does not mean zero acceleration.","answer":"zero velocity, and acceleration g downwards"},"phy-11-03-m10":{"idea":"Relative velocity compares two moving bodies from the point of view of one of them.","steps":["The velocity of A relative to B is the velocity of A as seen from B.","It is found by subtracting B's velocity from A's, as vectors.","v_AB = v_A − v_B."],"others":"The vector sum v_A + v_B is not a relative velocity. The difference of speeds loses the directions. v_B − v_A is the velocity of B relative to A, which is the reverse.","trap":"Relative velocity of A with respect to B = A minus B, with signs and directions.","answer":"v_A − v_B, the vector difference of their velocities"},"phy-11-03-m11":{"idea":"Moving in opposite directions, the speeds add for the relative speed.","steps":["Take A as +20 m/s and B as −30 m/s.","v_AB = v_A − v_B = 20 − (−30).","= 50 m/s."],"others":"600 m/s is the product of the speeds, which has no meaning here. 10 m/s is the answer for trains going in the same direction. 25 m/s is just the average of the speeds.","trap":"Same direction: subtract the speeds. Opposite direction: add them.","answer":"50 m/s"},"phy-11-03-m12":{"idea":"Only gravity acts on a projectile, and gravity is vertical. So the horizontal motion is steady.","steps":["No horizontal force acts, once the body is thrown.","So the horizontal acceleration is zero.","Hence the horizontal velocity stays the same: u cos θ."],"others":"It does not decrease, because nothing slows it down. It does not increase on the way down, since gravity has no horizontal part. It is not zero at the top: only the vertical part is zero there.","trap":"Horizontal and vertical motions are independent.","answer":"remains constant, as no horizontal force acts"},"phy-11-03-m13":{"idea":"The time of flight is how long the vertical motion takes to go up and come back down to the same level.","steps":["The vertical velocity at the start is u sin θ.","Going up takes u sin θ / g. Coming down takes the same time.","Total T = 2u sin θ / g."],"others":"u² sin 2θ / g is the range, not a time. u sin θ / g is only the time to reach the top. 2u cos θ / g uses the horizontal component, which does not set the time in the air.","trap":"Time depends on the vertical component. Range depends on both.","answer":"2u sin θ/g"},"phy-11-03-m14":{"idea":"The range is R = u² sin 2θ / g. It is greatest when sin 2θ is greatest.","steps":["The largest value of sin 2θ is 1.","This needs 2θ = 90°.","So θ = 45°."],"others":"At 60° and at 30° sin 2θ = 0.866, which is less than 1. At 90° the body goes straight up and the range is zero.","trap":"Complementary angles (such as 30° and 60°) give equal ranges, and 45° gives the longest.","answer":"45°"},"phy-11-03-m15":{"idea":"Angles that add up to 90° give the same range.","steps":["R = u² sin 2θ / g.","For 30°: sin 60°. For 60°: sin 120°.","sin 60° = sin 120° = 0.866, so the ranges are equal."],"others":"The higher path at 60° does not mean a longer range, since it spends the time going up. A lower angle does not guarantee a longer range either. The ratio of the ranges is 1 : 1, not 1 : 2.","trap":"The 60° throw goes higher, but the 30° throw goes faster along the ground. They land at the same place.","answer":"equal, as sin 60° = sin 120°"},"phy-11-03-m16":{"idea":"At the top, only the vertical velocity is zero. The horizontal velocity is still there.","steps":["The vertical component u sin θ falls to zero at the top.","The horizontal component u cos θ never changes.","So the velocity at the top is horizontal, with size u cos θ."],"others":"The vertical value u sin θ is the starting vertical speed, not the speed at the top. The body does not stop, because it still moves sideways. The speed cannot be zero while the horizontal part is u cos θ.","trap":"Speed at the top is u cos θ, not zero (unless θ = 90°).","answer":"horizontal, and equal to u cos θ"},"phy-11-03-m17":{"idea":"In uniform circular motion, the speed is constant but the direction keeps changing.","steps":["Velocity is a vector, so a changing direction means a changing velocity.","A changing velocity means that there is an acceleration.","This acceleration points towards the centre."],"others":"The acceleration is not along the velocity, which would change the speed. The velocity is not constant, because its direction changes. The acceleration is not zero, since the velocity changes.","trap":"Constant speed does not mean constant velocity.","answer":"its velocity changes, and its acceleration points to the centre"},"phy-11-03-m18":{"idea":"Linear speed is the arc length per second. Angular speed is the angle per second.","steps":["In a time t, the arc length is s = rθ.","Divide by t: v = r × (θ/t).","So v = ωr."],"others":"v = ω/r has r in the wrong place. ω = vr also puts r on the wrong side. v = ωr² has an extra power of r.","trap":"Check units: ω is in rad/s, r in m, so ωr is in m/s.","answer":"v = ωr"},"phy-11-03-m19":{"idea":"The centripetal acceleration points to the centre and has the size v²/r.","steps":["Velocity changes direction at a rate v × ω.","Since ω = v/r, the acceleration is v × v/r = v²/r.","It acts towards the centre."],"others":"v/r² and vr do not have the units of acceleration. v²r grows with r, but a larger circle gives a smaller acceleration. 'Away from the centre' is the direction of the centrifugal effect, not of the actual acceleration.","trap":"Check units: v²/r = m²/s² / m = m/s².","answer":"v²/r, towards the centre"},"phy-11-03-m20":{"idea":"Centripetal force F = mv²/r, so it goes with the square of the speed.","steps":["F = mv²/r.","With the speed doubled: F′ = m(2v)²/r = 4mv²/r.","So F becomes 4 times as large."],"others":"It does not stay unchanged, because F depends on v. Twice as large would mean F is proportional to v, but it goes with v². Half is the wrong direction, since a faster body needs more force.","trap":"Twice the speed means four times the force. That is why sharp bends need slower speeds.","answer":"four times as large"},"phy-11-03-m21":{"idea":"Centripetal force is a name for the net force towards the centre. It is not a new force.","steps":["The only real force on the stone in a horizontal circle (ignoring weight) is the tension.","The tension acts along the string, to the centre.","So the tension supplies the centripetal force."],"others":"The pull of the stone on the hand acts on the hand, not on the stone. The weight acts downwards, not towards the centre. The centripetal force is not an extra force on top of the tension.","trap":"Never draw centripetal force as a separate force in a free-body diagram.","answer":"the tension in the string, which is a real force, directed to the centre"},"phy-11-03-m22":{"idea":"The car needs a force towards the centre of the curve. On a flat road only friction can give it.","steps":["Weight and the normal reaction act vertically and cancel each other.","The engine pushes along the road, not towards the centre.","The sideways friction of the tyres on the road gives the centripetal force."],"others":"The weight acts downwards, so it cannot turn the car. The normal reaction acts upwards, so it cannot turn the car either. The engine's force acts along the road, which changes the speed along the path.","trap":"On an icy road, friction is small and the car slides straight out of the curve.","answer":"the force of friction between the tyres and the road"},"phy-11-03-m23":{"idea":"The bob does not move up or down, so the vertical forces balance.","steps":["The forces on the bob are the weight (down) and the tension (along the string).","The vertical part of the tension is T cos θ.","T cos θ = mg, so it balances the weight."],"others":"The horizontal part of the tension is the one that supplies the centripetal force. Air force is not part of this simple model. The two parts of the tension are at right angles, so they cannot balance each other.","trap":"Vertical: T cos θ = mg. Horizontal: T sin θ = mv²/r.","answer":"the weight of the bob"},"phy-11-03-n01":{"idea":"With constant acceleration, use v = u + at.","steps":["u = 5 m/s, a = 2 m/s², t = 6 s.","v = 5 + 2 × 6.","v = 17.0 m/s."],"trap":"The velocity rises by 2 m/s every second. After 6 s it has risen by 12 m/s.","answer":"17.0 m/s"},"phy-11-03-n02":{"idea":"Use s = ut + ½at², with a negative because of the retardation.","steps":["u = 10 m/s, a = −2 m/s², t = 4 s.","s = 10 × 4 + ½ × (−2) × 4².","s = 40 − 16 = 24.0 m."],"trap":"The car stops at t = 5 s, so it is still moving at 4 s. The formula is valid here.","answer":"24.0 m"},"phy-11-03-n03":{"idea":"Use v² = u² + 2as with v = 0.","steps":["u = 20 m/s, a = −5 m/s², v = 0.","0 = 400 + 2(−5)s, so 10 s = 400.","s = 40.0 m."],"trap":"Check with time: t = 4 s and average speed = 10 m/s, so s = 40 m.","answer":"40.0 m"},"phy-11-03-n04":{"idea":"Average speed is the total distance divided by the total time. It is not the simple average of the speeds.","steps":["Let the one-way distance be d. Time going = d/60. Time returning = d/40.","Total time = d(1/60 + 1/40) = d × 5/120 = d/24.","Average speed = 2d / (d/24) = 48.0 km/h."],"trap":"The answer is not 50, since the car spends more time at the slower speed.","answer":"48.0 km/h"},"phy-11-03-n05":{"idea":"Use the range formula R = u² sin 2θ / g.","steps":["u = 20 m/s, θ = 30°, g = 10 m/s².","sin 2θ = sin 60° = 0.866.","R = 400 × 0.866 / 10 = 34.64 m."],"trap":"The range at 45° would be 40 m, the largest. 34.64 m is smaller, as it should be.","answer":"34.64 m"},"phy-11-03-n06":{"idea":"Use the maximum height formula H = u² sin²θ / 2g.","steps":["u sin θ = 20 × 0.5 = 10 m/s.","H = (10)² / (2 × 10).","H = 5.00 m."],"trap":"Check by free fall: from the top, the ball falls 5 m in 1 s, since ½ × 10 × 1² = 5.","answer":"5.00 m"},"phy-11-03-n07":{"idea":"The time of flight is T = 2u sin θ / g.","steps":["u sin θ = 10 m/s.","T = 2 × 10 / 10.","T = 2.00 s."],"trap":"Going up takes 1 s and coming down takes 1 s.","answer":"2.00 s"},"phy-11-03-n08":{"idea":"Relative velocity is a vector difference. East and north are at right angles.","steps":["v_A = 20 m/s east, v_B = 15 m/s north.","v_AB = v_A − v_B. The two parts are at right angles.","Size = √(20² + 15²) = √625 = 25.0 m/s."],"trap":"20-15-25 is a 3-4-5 triangle scaled by 5.","answer":"25.0 m/s"},"phy-11-03-n09":{"idea":"Centripetal acceleration a = v²/r.","steps":["v = 10 m/s, r = 5 m.","a = 10² / 5.","a = 20.0 m/s²."],"trap":"Units: m²/s² divided by m gives m/s².","answer":"20.0 m/s²"},"phy-11-03-n10":{"idea":"Change revolutions per minute to rad/s first, then use a = ω²r.","steps":["ω = 120 × 2π / 60 = 4π rad/s = 12.566 rad/s.","a = ω²r = (12.566)² × 0.50.","a = 157.91 × 0.50 = 78.96 m/s²."],"trap":"Do not use 120 directly in the formula. Convert to rad/s first.","answer":"78.96 m/s²"},"phy-11-03-n11":{"idea":"On a flat curve, friction gives the centripetal force. The largest friction is μmg.","steps":["At the limit, mv²/r = μmg.","The mass cancels: v² = μ g r = 0.5 × 10 × 20 = 100.","v = 10.0 m/s."],"trap":"The result does not depend on the mass of the car.","answer":"10.0 m/s"},"phy-11-03-n12":{"idea":"For a conical pendulum, T = 2π √(l cos θ / g), with θ measured from the vertical.","steps":["Vertical: T cos θ = mg. Horizontal: T sin θ = mω²r, with r = l sin θ.","This gives ω² = g/(l cos θ).","T = 2π √(1.0 × 0.5 / 9.8) = 2π × 0.2259 = 1.419 s."],"trap":"A larger angle from the vertical gives a shorter period, since cos θ becomes smaller.","answer":"1.419 s"},"phy-11-03-n13":{"idea":"A dropped stone starts from rest, so s = ½gt².","steps":["s = 45 m, u = 0, g = 10 m/s².","45 = ½ × 10 × t², so t² = 9.","t = 3.00 s."],"trap":"Check the final speed: v = gt = 30 m/s, and v² = 2gs = 900. Both agree.","answer":"3.00 s"}};
